CC0 1.0 Universal

Statement of Purpose

The laws of most jurisdictions throughout the world automatically confer
exclusive Copyright and Neighbourhood Rights ownership in any original work
of authorship (the "Work") created by a creator. Certain owners wish to
permanently relinquish those rights and commit the Work to the world's
public domain, without any conditions or restrictions whatsoever.

These owners may do so legitimately in jurisdictions that support this
concept, including but not limited to the United States of America, the
European Union, the United Kingdom, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, the
Republic of South Africa, Argentina, Brazil, Mexico, Chile, Colombia,
Peru, and much of Central and South America. For other jurisdictions,
including but not limited to much of Asia, the Middle East, and Africa,
the use of the Work and the exercise of rights under the Work is subject
to, and not exempted from, applicable copyright and neighbouring rights
laws.

The person dedicating the Work to the public domain may do so to the full
extent allowed by applicable law; cannot be held liable for any direct,
indirect, special, incidental, or consequential damages resulting from the
use or misuse of the Work.

To the extent the Work is licensed under the legal equivalent of the
Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Public Domain Dedication, the person
dedicating the Work is in no way making any warranties or conditions
concerning the Work, and the exercise of the rights granted in the Work
is done at the dedicatee's own risk.

